When I was in high school I worked at the hardware store on Main Street. The owner, Mister Hollis, was strict about two things. You had to know where every bolt was kept, and you never let a customer leave without asking about their family. I hated it at first. Then one winter an older woman came in for a furnace filter, and I asked about her family the way he taught me. She told me her husband had died that week. We talked for half an hour. Before she left she said I was the first person who had asked her anything all day. I understood then why he cared so much about that question.
